   "source": [
    "### 7.2 Discussion\n",
    "\n",
    "The best configuration overall — for the practical goal of finding Relevant economic articles — is the hyperparameter-tuned, class-balanced LinearSVC (Experiment 2), or, among the untouched baselines, Multinomial Naive Bayes at 5,000 features. Both outperform the plain (unbalanced) discriminative classifiers on recall and F1 for the Relevant class, because they counteract the model's natural bias toward the majority Not Relevant class.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Is accuracy alone sufficient?** No. Because roughly 82% of articles are Not Relevant, a trivial classifier that always predicts \"Not Relevant\" scores about 82% accuracy while providing zero business value. Several of the best-accuracy configurations in this study in fact have the *worst* recall on the Relevant class. Precision, recall, F1-score and the confusion matrix — evaluated specifically for the minority/target class — are the metrics that actually reflect whether the system is useful for its intended purpose.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "45a6d917",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 8. Conclusion\n",
    "\n",
    "This notebook implemented a complete Scikit-learn text-classification pipeline for the Economic News Article Tone and Relevance dataset. Pre-processing, Bag-of-Words feature extraction at three vocabulary sizes, and three classifier families were implemented and evaluated with full confusion matrices. Class imbalance was shown to materially affect model behaviour, and class-weighting plus hyperparameter tuning meaningfully improved recall and F1-score on the minority Relevant class relative to an unbalanced baseline. F1-score / recall on the Relevant class — not raw accuracy — is the correct criterion for selecting a deployment model on this imbalanced dataset.\n",
    "\n",
   ]
